<% content_for :title, "The Science of Flatness | wordspeltwrong.io" %>
<article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl space-y-8">
  <header>
    <%= link_to "← Back to your organisation", root_path, class: "font-semibold text-brand-700 underline" %>
    <p class="mt-8 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-flat-500">Applied Pancake Dynamics</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">The Science of Flatness</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600">An authoritative framework for the measurement and continuous improvement of organisational maturity.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Corporate philosophy</h2>
    <p>Traditional organisations are built from the top down. Progressive organisations are built from the bottom up. At wordspeltwrong.io, we believe both approaches suffer from excessive verticality.</p>
    <p>That's why we flipped the traditional management hierarchy and began systematically eliminating its thickness.</p>
    <p class="font-bold text-brand-700">Flipping hierarchies. Flattening organisations. One percent at a time.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Asymptotic flattening</h2>
    <p>Organisational flatness is measured in millipancakes (mPc). After you joined wordspeltwrong, our People Team carried out a comprehensive assessment of your organisation to establish its initial hierarchical thickness, <em>T</em><sub>0</sub>. Each deliberate operation eliminates 1% of the residual hierarchical thickness: the distance from the current index to the Crêpe Limit.</p>
    <p class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-brand-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm">flatness = 1,000 − <em>T</em><sub>0</sub> × 0.99<sup>deliberate flattenings − entropy events</sup></p>
    <p>Under <strong>Pascal's Pancake Principle</strong>, each operation removes the same proportion of a progressively smaller residual thickness. The absolute improvement therefore diminishes even as our commitment remains constant.</p>
    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="approach-title approach-desc" class="w-full">
        <title id="approach-title">Approaching the Crêpe Limit</title>
        <desc id="approach-desc">The flatness curve rises from the assessed starting point towards, but never reaches, 1,000 mPc as effective flattenings increase.</desc>
        <path d="M70 20V180H570" fill="none" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
        <path d="M70 30H570" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>
        <path d="M70 165 C120 125 170 95 220 75 S340 42 570 32" fill="none" stroke="#6d28d9" stroke-width="4"/>
        <text x="75" y="24" font-size="13" fill="#9a3412">Crêpe Limit: 1,000 mPc</text>
        <text x="40" y="169" font-size="13" fill="#475569">T₀</text>
        <text x="300" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#475569">Effective flattening count →</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-slate-500">Figure 1. Asymptotic approach to the Crêpe Limit.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">The Crêpe Limit</h2>
    <p>The Crêpe Limit represents the theoretical state of an organisation possessing no residual hierarchical thickness. Whilst mathematically approachable, it remains unattainable, necessitating a permanent commitment to continuous flattening.</p>
    <p>It is a fundamental constant in Applied Pancake Dynamics: <strong>1,000 mPc</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">The Law of Hierarchical Entropy</h2>
    <p>Hierarchical Entropy is the naturally occurring tendency of organisations to develop additional layers of management over time. Even in otherwise stable organisational environments, spontaneous hierarchical regrowth has been observed at remarkably consistent seven-day intervals, independent of stakeholder activity.</p>
    <p>Each complete seven-day period since establishment reverses one deliberate flattening. Daily deliberate flattening can occur at seven times the frequency of spontaneous thickening, enabling sustained progress towards the Crêpe Limit. Seven daily operations and one entropy event produce six net operations.</p>
    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="entropy-title entropy-desc" class="w-full">
        <title id="entropy-title">Daily flattening and weekly entropy</title>
        <desc id="entropy-desc">Daily flattening raises the effective count, while weekly entropy produces a small downward step. The net trend remains upward.</desc>
        <path d="M70 20V180H570" fill="none" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
        <path d="M70 170 L95 160 120 150 145 140 170 130 195 120 220 110 245 100 245 110 270 100 295 90 320 80 345 70 370 60 395 50 420 40 420 50 445 40 470 30" fill="none" stroke="#6d28d9" stroke-width="4"/>
        <text x="225" y="129" font-size="12" fill="#9a3412">Entropy</text>
        <text x="400" y="70" font-size="12" fill="#9a3412">Entropy</text>
        <text x="8" y="25" font-size="13" fill="#475569">Net</text>
        <text x="300" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#475569">Days since establishment →</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-slate-500">Figure 2. Daily deliberate flattening with weekly Hierarchical Entropy.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="font-bold text-brand-700">Left to their own devices, organisations thicken.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">The Principle of Unbounded Dysfunction</h2>
    <p>Whilst organisational perfection is bounded by the Crêpe Limit, organisational dysfunction is subject to no equivalent constraint. Research in Applied Pancake Dynamics indicates that there is no theoretical limit to the number of unnecessary management layers an organisation may develop.</p>
    <p>As the effective count approaches positive infinity, flatness approaches 1,000 mPc; as it approaches negative infinity, flatness approaches negative infinity. Consequently, whilst absolute flatness remains unattainable, there is always scope for an organisation to become worse.</p>
    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="dysfunction-title dysfunction-desc" class="w-full">
        <title id="dysfunction-title">The unbounded flatness curve</title>
        <desc id="dysfunction-desc">The curve extends downwards without limit for negative effective counts and approaches 1,000 mPc for positive counts.</desc>
        <path d="M70 20V205H570" fill="none" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
        <path d="M70 30H570 M70 115H570" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>
        <path d="M95 200 C130 150 165 100 220 65 S340 35 570 31" fill="none" stroke="#6d28d9" stroke-width="4"/>
        <text x="8" y="34" font-size="13" fill="#475569">1,000</text>
        <text x="40" y="119" font-size="13" fill="#475569">0</text>
        <text x="80" y="135" font-size="12" fill="#9a3412">Subpancake territory</text>
        <text x="300" y="240" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#475569">Negative ← Effective count → Positive</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-slate-500">Figure 3. Organisational flatness across both domains.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="font-bold text-brand-700">Perfection has a limit. Dysfunction does not.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Organisational maturity classifications</h2>
    <p>The following framework defines organisational maturity in millipancakes. Negative flatness is collectively known as <strong>subpancake territory</strong>, or the <strong>bread domain</strong>.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead><tr class="border-b border-brand-100"><th class="p-2">Flatness (mPc)</th><th class="p-2">Classification</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p-2">1,000</td><td class="p-2">Theoretical perfect pancake (unattainable)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">950–&lt;1,000</td><td class="p-2">Ultra-thin crêpe</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">800–&lt;950</td><td class="p-2">French crêpe</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">600–&lt;800</td><td class="p-2">British pancake</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">400–&lt;600</td><td class="p-2">Drop scone</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">200–&lt;400</td><td class="p-2">Scotch pancake</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">0–&lt;200</td><td class="p-2">American pancake</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">−500–&lt;0</td><td class="p-2">Ciabatta</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-2">Below −500</td><td class="p-2">Loaf</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Organisations exhibiting negative flatness cease to satisfy the minimum requirements for pancake classification and are instead classified within the bread domain. Whilst ciabatta-class organisations retain some degree of horizontal potential, loaf-class organisations exhibit substantial vertical management structures. There is no theoretical limit to the thickness of a loaf-class organisation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Scientific terminology</h2>
    <dl class="space-y-3">
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Millipancake (mPc)</dt><dd>The standard unit of organisational flatness.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">The Crêpe Limit</dt><dd>The theoretical upper bound of organisational flatness.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Pascal's Pancake Principle</dt><dd>The principle of diminishing returns in organisational flattening.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Residual hierarchical thickness</dt><dd>The remaining distance to the Crêpe Limit.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Hierarchical inversion</dt><dd>The process of flipping a traditional management hierarchy.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Asymptotic flattening</dt><dd>The continuous process of approaching the Crêpe Limit.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Hierarchical Entropy</dt><dd>The naturally occurring tendency towards increasing hierarchical thickness.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Organisational recurvature</dt><dd>The observable reduction in organisational flatness resulting from Hierarchical Entropy.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">The Principle of Unbounded Dysfunction</dt><dd>The absence of a theoretical lower bound on organisational flatness.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Subpancake territory</dt><dd>The domain of negative organisational flatness.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">The bread domain</dt><dd>The collective classification of organisations that no longer qualify as pancakes.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-bold">Applied Pancake Dynamics</dt><dd>The scientific discipline overseen by the Chief Flatness Officer.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <p class="text-center text-xl font-bold text-brand-700">Absolute flatness is not a destination. It's a journey.</p>
</article>
